  url = {https://hal.science/hal-04667390},
  urldate = {2026-05-11},
  abstract = {The swift expansion of digital media has fueled advancements in multimedia creation, especially in Music Anime Douga (MAD), which integrates animation and music. Producing MADs typically demands substantial manual effort, particularly for crafting key frames. While current approaches like GANs and transformers perform well in text-to-video synthesis, they fall short in providing the artistic control essential for MADs, often overlooking the pivotal hand-drawn frames that form the videos' visual core. This paper presents a new framework designed to generate high-quality videos from text-image pairs, addressing this shortfall. Our multi-modal system deciphers both narrative and visual inputs, producing smooth video outputs by combining text-to-video and image-to-video synthesis. This method improves artistic control, maintaining the creator's vision while making the production process more efficient. Our framework opens up MAD production to a wider range of artists, fostering greater creativity and innovation. We offer a thorough review of existing research, elaborate on our model's structure, and validate its efficacy through experiments. This study sets the stage for future progress in AI-assisted MAD creation.},
  langid = {english},
